WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:13.715
Odysseus and his men smeared ash upon their faces and raised their whitest sail.

2
00:00:14.255 --> 00:00:15.516
They waited for the wind to stir.

3
00:00:15.856 --> 00:00:19.537
But the wind of death is not an earthly wind; it cannot stir the leaves on a tree

4
00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:21.177
or ruffle the hair on a person's head.

5
00:00:21.918 --> 00:00:24.018
Circe watched Odysseus and his men depart.

6
00:00:24.458 --> 00:00:29.280
Before they left the land of the living, she called out, "Odysseus, remember to make the sacrifice as

7
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.020
I instructed you.

8
00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:32.910
A mistake could trap you in Hades, the land of the dead."

9
00:00:33.591 --> 00:00:37.134
The ship seemed to sail through the night, but it was a windless and starless night.

10
00:00:37.694 --> 00:00:40.396
Without warning, the ship stopped as if the ship had become beached.

11
00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:45.721
Odysseus and his men found themselves in a dark and featureless place that made each of them long

12
00:00:45.761 --> 00:00:46.822
for the warmth of the sun.

13
00:00:47.963 --> 00:00:53.207
Not trusting his men, Odysseus unloaded what he needed and prepared the sacrifice himself just as Circe had

14
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:57.951
instructed. He spilled the blood of a ewe and a ram upon the colorless ground, and then said

15
00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:58.832
a prayer to the dead.

16
00:00:59.452 --> 00:01:01.414
Soon the shadows of the dead began to arrive.

17
00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:04.016
Odysseus recognized many of the dead that appeared.

18
00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:08.900
When he saw his mother walking toward him he almost faltered in his appointed task, because Circe had

19
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.224
instructed him to keep the blood and bodies of the ram and ewe away from the dead until

20
00:01:13.264 --> 00:01:14.085
Tiresias appeared.

21
00:01:14.946 --> 00:01:19.309
After forcing his mother back at the point of his sword, Tiresias arrived and feasted on the life

22
00:01:19.349 --> 00:01:20.430
that had been sacrificed.

23
00:01:21.031 --> 00:01:22.032
When he finished, he spoke.

24
00:01:22.052 --> 00:01:25.054
"Odysseus, you have a hard journey ahead of you.

25
00:01:25.755 --> 00:01:30.067
But if you listen to me and heed my warnings, you alone shall make it back to Ithaca."

26
00:01:31.147 --> 00:01:33.569
Odysseus listened as Tiresias revealed the future.

27
00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.394
When Tiresias was finished, other shadows feasted on the ram and ewe that had been sacrificed and then

28
00:01:39.414 --> 00:01:39.895
they spoke.

29
00:01:40.575 --> 00:01:46.260
Odysseus listened to their tales but as the dead were more numerous than ants, Odysseus soon felt overwhelmed

30
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.241
by their presence and ran back to his ship.

31
00:01:48.922 --> 00:01:51.204
He shouted to his men, "Row for your lives!"

32
00:01:51.884 --> 00:01:55.768
The men needed no other encouragement; they rowed for their lives and escaped from the land of the

33
00:01:55.808 --> 00:01:59.921
dead. They returned to the land of the living, like the sun returns at the dawn of a

34
00:01:59.961 --> 00:02:00.402
new day.

35
00:02:01.002 --> 00:02:03.303
With the sun in his hair, Odysseus addressed his men.

36
00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:07.805
"We are free of Hades, but Tiresias has warned me that much danger lies ahead.

37
00:02:08.565 --> 00:02:10.286
Our first danger is the Sirens."

38
00:02:11.366 --> 00:02:15.668
Odysseus told his men about the Sirens—that they sing a song that lures sailors to their deaths.

39
00:02:16.128 --> 00:02:19.330
He then instructed his men to put wax in their ears and to tie him to the main

40
00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.751
mast. "Ignore me," he said.

41
00:02:21.771 --> 00:02:24.332
"Do not stop rowing until we are past the island."

42
00:02:24.788 --> 00:02:28.912
His men obeyed, and although Odysseus did everything in his power to escape the ropes that bound him,

43
00:02:29.332 --> 00:02:34.056
they rowed and Odysseus remained firmly tied to the mast as the Sirens' song urged him to jump

44
00:02:34.096 --> 00:02:34.596
to his death.

45
00:02:35.437 --> 00:02:37.499
More danger lurked ahead: Scylla and Charybdis.

46
00:02:37.919 --> 00:02:42.623
Scylla was a six-headed monster that hid in a cave, and Charybdis was a monster that sucked in

47
00:02:42.643 --> 00:02:44.184
the sea and created a whirlpool.

48
00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:48.308
Odysseus told his men about Charybdis, but he didn't warn them about Scylla.

49
00:02:48.808 --> 00:02:53.072
"Better to lose six men to Scylla than everyone to Charybdis," Tiresias had warned.

50
00:02:54.056 --> 00:02:56.178
Odysseus took the rudder and his men were at the oars.

51
00:02:56.718 --> 00:03:00.682
The men were rowing mightily when they saw Charybdis on their left sucking in and spitting out the

52
00:03:00.742 --> 00:03:02.903
sea through her cruel fang-filled mouth.

53
00:03:03.584 --> 00:03:07.547
While the men watched Charybdis fearfully, Scylla suddenly appeared and ate men at once.

54
00:03:08.268 --> 00:03:12.851
Odysseus heard his men yell, and he heard Charybdis sucking and spitting, but he looked neither right nor

55
00:03:12.891 --> 00:03:17.215
left. He kept his eyes and his rudder steady, and he steered his ship and men as safely

56
00:03:17.255 --> 00:03:19.036
as possible between the two monsters.

57
00:03:19.737 --> 00:03:23.060
After they made it past Scylla and Charybdis, Odysseus spoke to his men again.

58
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.941
"There is one more danger left.

59
00:03:25.441 --> 00:03:30.084
As tired and grieving as I know you are, we must not stop at this next island, the

60
00:03:30.184 --> 00:03:30.964
Island of the Sun."

61
00:03:31.565 --> 00:03:34.867
The men were sick with grief, and exhausted from the dangers they had already faced.

62
00:03:34.887 --> 00:03:37.948
"We cannot go any farther," they moaned.

63
00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:40.590
"Can't we just stop on the island and sleep?"

64
00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.873
Odysseus knew that he shouldn't agree, because stopping would end in ruin, but his men looked exhausted.

65
00:03:45.893 --> 00:03:48.755
"We cannot row through the night," they pleaded.

66
00:03:49.335 --> 00:03:54.122
Finally Odysseus agreed, but he said, "If we stop on this island, each of you must swear not

67
00:03:54.142 --> 00:03:55.363
to touch the cattle or sheep.

68
00:03:55.803 --> 00:03:57.804
We must eat our own food and nothing more."

69
00:03:58.365 --> 00:03:59.886
The men agreed, and they went ashore.

70
00:04:00.286 --> 00:04:05.269
For the next twenty days, terrible storms raged, and it was impossible for Odysseus and his men to

71
00:04:05.329 --> 00:04:09.052
leave. On the twentieth day, Odysseus went alone to pray to the gods.

72
00:04:09.472 --> 00:04:13.375
The men were tired of eating fish, and they looked hungrily at the cattle that roamed freely over

73
00:04:13.415 --> 00:04:13.855
the island.

74
00:04:14.535 --> 00:04:15.996
Eurylocus saw the men's desire.

75
00:04:16.337 --> 00:04:19.939
He convinced them that it would be all right to kill the cows if they offered some as

76
00:04:19.999 --> 00:04:21.160
sacrifice to the gods.

77
00:04:22.416 --> 00:04:25.177
Apollo saw his cattle being eaten, and he immediately went to Zeus.

78
00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:29.178
"Father," he said, "I want vengeance for this wrong."

79
00:04:30.238 --> 00:04:32.459
"You shall have it," answered Zeus.

80
00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:34.600
"Odysseus was warned.

81
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.041
I shall blast their ship with my lightning bolt when they set sail."

82
00:04:38.761 --> 00:04:40.822
Odysseus returned and saw what his men had done.

83
00:04:41.302 --> 00:04:42.242
He knew they were doomed.

84
00:04:42.842 --> 00:04:45.243
A few days later the weather cleared and they set sail.

85
00:04:45.523 --> 00:04:48.124
They had not gone far when a single menacing cloud appeared.

86
00:04:48.956 --> 00:04:52.977
Odysseus suspected that Zeus was about to destroy them so he tied himself to the rudder.

87
00:04:53.497 --> 00:04:56.858
When the bolt struck, the boat was shattered, but Odysseus escaped destruction.

88
00:04:57.458 --> 00:05:01.059
Several days passed until finally Odysseus came ashore on Calypso's island.

89
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.961
Odysseus, shipless and lost, remained stranded on Calypso's island for five years.

90
00:05:06.581 --> 00:05:08.881
Calypso, a minor goddess, treated him kindly.

91
00:05:09.302 --> 00:05:13.143
Odysseus did what he could for her, but he longed to return home, and he prayed to Athena

92
00:05:13.223 --> 00:05:14.123
nightly for her help.

93
00:05:14.803 --> 00:05:18.064
Finally one night Athena heard his prayers, and she approached her father.

94
00:05:18.656 --> 00:05:22.797
"Great Zeus, Odysseus has suffered enough; it is time for him to return home.

95
00:05:23.517 --> 00:05:24.918
Please release him from his prison."

96
00:05:24.938 --> 00:05:28.219
"Yes, my wise daughter, it is time.

97
00:05:28.719 --> 00:05:30.579
I will send Hermes with instructions."

98
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.660
Hermes arrived and Calypso recognized him immediately.

99
00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.121
In annoyance she said, "What does Great Zeus want of me?"

100
00:05:37.141 --> 00:05:39.922
"Your obedience," replied Hermes.

101
00:05:40.442 --> 00:05:42.483
"I have grown fond of Odysseus," she said.

102
00:05:42.503 --> 00:05:44.143
"I don't want him to leave."

103
00:05:44.163 --> 00:05:47.324
"It is not your choice to make," said Hermes.

104
00:05:48.032 --> 00:05:49.474
"It is time for him to go home."

105
00:05:49.494 --> 00:05:52.058
"I have no ship," insisted Calypso.

106
00:05:52.078 --> 00:05:56.584
"He shall build a raft, and Zeus will see to the rest," replied Hermes.

107
00:05:57.044 --> 00:05:58.767
"All you need to do is to obey."

108
00:05:59.387 --> 00:06:00.108
And he was gone.
